Section 3: Authorization & Resource-Level Security
Server-Side Enforcement: The agent must implement a meaningful
authorization model that is strictly enforced on the server. Relying purely on
hiding buttons or controls in the frontend user interface is a critical failure.
PDF + 3
Authentication & Role Mapping: Implement a simple token-based login utilizing
the seeded test users. Build backend middleware or dependencies that load the
authenticated user's specific role (e.g., Viewer, Member, Lead, Admin) for each
team they belong to.
PDF + 3
Resource-Level Scoping: Every single query, read, and write action must be
scoped to the item's associated team and the user's corresponding role within
that team. Centralize this logic into a single permission-check function used
across endpoints.
PDF + 2
Strict Access Control: If a user attempts to read, edit, or claim a work item
belonging to a team they are not a member of, the backend must immediately
reject the request and return a 403 Forbidden or 404 Not Found error.
PDF
Role-Restricted Workflows: Certain workflow transitions must be restricted by
role. For example, progressing an item to an "Approved" or "Resolved" state
should specifically require the user to hold a "Lead" role for that item's team.